{"parentUuid":"992b04b2-9cef-4f5f-8cf6-d5d3737fdcc6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012Yf5F478k1tcuJC9rp6MxG","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CDCL SAT solver cache miss profile watch list data layout 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - sukrutrao/SAT-Solver-CDCL: A simple SAT solver based on the CDCL algorithm · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/sukrutrao/SAT-Solver-CDCL\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - sgomber/CDCL-SAT: A SAT Solver based on CDCL (Conflict Driven Clause Learning) implemented in python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/sgomber/CDCL-SAT\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-driven clause learning - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict-driven_clause_learning\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - arminbiere/cadical: CaDiCaL SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arminbiere/cadical\"},{\"title\":\"CDCL SAT Solvers & SAT-Based Problem Solving\",\"url\":\"https://cse.usf.edu/~haozheng/teach/cda5416/slides/intro-sat.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-driven clause learning (CDCL) SAT solvers — CS-E3220: Propositional satisfiability and SAT solvers documentation\",\"url\":\"https://users.aalto.fi/~tjunttil/2020-DP-AUT/notes-sat/cdcl.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ruberith/CDCLsat.jl: CDCL SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ruberith/CDCLsat.jl\"},{\"title\":\"cdcl-algorithm · GitHub Topics · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/topics/cdcl-algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"Cache Performance of SAT Solvers: a Case Study for Efficient Implementation of Algorithms | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220944485_Cache_Performance_of_SAT_Solvers_a_Case_Study_for_Efficient_Implementation_of_Algorithms\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - z11i/pysat: Simple SAT solver with CDCL implemented in Python\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/z11i/pysat\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about CDCL SAT solvers and some performance optimization aspects:\n\n## CDCL SAT Solvers Overview\n\nConflict-driven clause learning (CDCL) is an algorithm for solving the Boolean satisfiability problem (SAT). Most state-of-the-art SAT solvers nowadays use the CDCL algorithm.\n\n## Cache Performance and Data Structures\n\nThe search results do contain research on cache performance optimization in CDCL solvers. A study discusses cache utilization in state-of-the-art SAT solvers, aiming to show how a resource-unaware SAT solver can be improved by utilizing the cache sensibly. For the analysis, the total cycles, the resource stall cycles, the L2 cache hits and the L2 cache misses are traced using sample based profiling.\n\n## Watch List Data Structure\n\nThe two-watched-literal scheme, enhanced with implementation details boosting its performance, emerged as the dominant method. This is a key data structure in CDCL solvers for efficient unit propagation.\n\n## Recent Developments\n\nCaDiCaL 2.0 was presented at the 26th International Conference on Computer-Aided Verification (CAV'24) with papers published in LNCS volume 14681, pages 133-152, Springer 2024.\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific recent research (2024-2025) that comprehensively addresses all aspects of your query, particularly regarding detailed cache miss profiles and watch list data layout optimizations in the 2024-2025 timeframe.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"2aeeb049-626a-4db8-8a17-f0f70aec6ebd","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:36.784Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"992b04b2-9cef-4f5f-8cf6-d5d3737fdcc6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"1b7627b3-5031-404e-aaec-00cc0cd8057b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018AgGr1PED3bVro7EYwgyLX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT propagator data structure cache aware OR-Tools Perron 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The CP-SAT-LP Solver Laurent Perron # Google, Paris, France Frédéric Didier #\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol280-cp2023/LIPIcs.CP.2023.3/LIPIcs.CP.2023.3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy.solvers.ortools — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/cp_optimizer/_modules/cpmpy/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT for scheduling Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://schedulingseminar.com/presentations/SchedulingSeminar_LaurentPerron.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Basic Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing Hybrid CP-SAT Search for Disjunctive Scheduling\",\"url\":\"https://hal.science/hal-04174800/document\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about CP-SAT propagators and OR-Tools by Laurent Perron. Here's what I found:\n\nThe CP-SAT-LP solver is developed by the Operations Research team at Google and is part of the OR-Tools open-source optimization suite. Laurent Perron is affiliated with Google, Paris, France.\n\nThe search results include several resources by Laurent Perron and colleagues on CP-SAT:\n\n1. **Academic Publication**: A paper on \"The CP-SAT-LP Solver\" by Laurent Perron, Frédéric Didier, and Steven Gay was presented at the Constraint Programming conference (CP 2023).\n\n2. **Propagation Design**: The linear relaxation is implemented as a propagator and potentially executed at every node in the search tree but only at lowest priority. Additionally, extending literal representation from 32 bits in most SAT solvers to 64 bits might negatively impact memory consumption and cache usage.\n\n3. **Constraint Propagation**: CP-SAT utilizes a dedicated domain-based propagator for constraints like add_all_different, enhancing efficiency beyond what is achievable with multiple != constraints.\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific details about cache-aware data structures for CP-SAT propagators in recent 2024-2025 publications. The most recent relevant academic work I found dates to CP 2023.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e45af34a-8ec6-42c1-9563-817d31b47d75","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:36.955Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1b7627b3-5031-404e-aaec-00cc0cd8057b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"716512ab-c8f3-423c-8ccd-c48060add83a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TBTJCaxELRSrd8QGqdjWcW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"roaring bitmap SAT solver clause representation bit-parallel propagation 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Roaring Bitmaps at the AI Paper Reading Club - Speaker Deck\",\"url\":\"https://speakerdeck.com/arto/roaring-bitmaps-at-the-ai-paper-reading-club\"},{\"title\":\"SAT solver - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SAT_solver\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Competition 2024\",\"url\":\"https://satcompetition.github.io/2024/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Efficient Certified Reasoning for Binarized Neural Networks Jiong Yang\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol341-sat2025/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.32/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.32.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Parallel CNF-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.columbia.edu/~sedwards/classes/2024/4995-fall/reports/sat-solver-report.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - urbanophile/awesome-sat-solvers: A curated collection of resources for the Boolean Satisfiability Problem (SAT). · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/urbanophile/awesome-sat-solvers\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - satcompetition/2024: SAT Competition 2024 · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/satcompetition/2024\"},{\"title\":\"Boolean Equi-propagation for Concise and Efficient SAT ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1402.0568\"},{\"title\":\"Fast Bit-Vector Satisfiability Peisen Yao\",\"url\":\"https://5hadowblad3.github.io/files/ISSTA20-Trident.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found some relevant information about SAT solvers and related techniques, though the specific combination of topics you queried (roaring bitmaps, SAT solver clause representation, and bit-parallel propagation) doesn't appear as a single unified research topic in recent publications.\n\nHere's what I found:\n\n**Roaring Bitmaps:**\nRoaring bitmaps were presented on May 4, 2026 at Frontier Tower, and there has been academic work on this topic. Research on \"Better Bitmap Performance with Roaring Bitmaps\" was published in Software: Practice and Experience in May 2016 and has been cited 300 times.\n\n**SAT Solver Development:**\nThe field of SAT solving continues to advance. The SAT solving area has seen tremendous progress, with problems that seemed out of reach a decade ago now being handled routinely, driven by new algorithms, better heuristics, and refined implementation techniques. Google's CP-SAT solver, part of OR-Tools, won gold medals at the Minizinc constraint programming competitions in editions 2018 up until 2025.\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific recent publications (2024-2025) that directly combine roaring bitmaps with SAT solver clause representation and bit-parallel propagation. You may want to check academic databases like arXiv, DBLP, or the SAT Conference proceedings directly for more specialized research on these specific techniques.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"462ad4d3-0203-430c-b5b3-cc4bba743967","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:38.146Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"716512ab-c8f3-423c-8ccd-c48060add83a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6e3f7024-e3e4-4f07-92c3-e2652cfc5972","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014gXs3hncFQ7DKt2NSaS6Ca","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT solver structure of arrays SoA AoS watch list performance benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Mapping code on Coarse Grained Reconfigurable Arrays using a SAT solver\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.02884\"},{\"title\":\"AoS and SoA - Algorithmica\",\"url\":\"https://en.algorithmica.org/hpc/cpu-cache/aos-soa/\"},{\"title\":\"Struct of Arrays (SoA) vs Array of Structs (AoS) - Performance - Julia Programming Language\",\"url\":\"https://discourse.julialang.org/t/struct-of-arrays-soa-vs-array-of-structs-aos/30015\"},{\"title\":\"Structure of Arrays (SoA) vs Array of Structures (AoS) in C++: A Deep Dive into Cache-Optimized Performance | by Lovekesh Azad | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@azad217/structure-of-arrays-soa-vs-array-of-structures-aos-in-c-a-deep-dive-into-cache-optimized-13847588232e\"},{\"title\":\"Structure of Arrays (SoA) vs Array of Structures (AoS) in C++: A Deep Dive into Cache-Optimized Performance\",\"url\":\"https://azad2171.github.io/soa_vs_aos_memory_layout_cpp/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - amgarrett09/SOA-vs-AOS: Benchmarks testing the relative performance of Stucture of Arrays vs Array of Structures architecture\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/amgarrett09/SOA-vs-AOS\"},{\"title\":\"Array of Structs and Struct of Arrays\",\"url\":\"https://hwisnu.bearblog.dev/array-of-structs-and-struct-of-arrays/\"},{\"title\":\"Analyzing SAT Solver Performance: From NP- ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/HSpapers/sat-anya.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - jake-arkinstall/soa: Structs of Arrays implementation in C++ · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/jake-arkinstall/soa\"},{\"title\":\"Memory Layout Transformations\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/memory-layout-transformations.html\"}]\n\nI've performed a web search for your query about SAT solver structure of arrays (SoA) and array of structures (AoS) with performance benchmarking. Here are the relevant findings:\n\n## General SoA vs AoS Concepts\n\nSoA means Struct of Arrays and AoS means Array of Structs. Changing your data layout can yield up to 40–60% performance improvement in real workloads.\n\n## Performance Characteristics\n\nIt is often beneficial to group together the data you need to fetch at the same time on the same or neighboring cache lines, which improves the spatial locality of your memory accesses, positively impacting the performance of memory-bound algorithms.\n\nIn every case, the SOA format is significantly faster for certain workloads. More specifically, for processing one field (such as summing bids only) or SIMD/vectorization opportunities, SoA is the recommended layout, while for processing all fields together or object-oriented modeling, AoS is recommended.\n\n## SAT Solver-Specific Results\n\nWhile the search results don't contain specific benchmarks about watch lists in SAT solvers, there is one paper discussing SAT solver performance: SAT-MapIt outperforms the state-of-the-art in almost 50% of the benchmarks by finding mappings with low II, and achieves higher quality mappings compared to existing state-of-the-art techniques.\n\nFor more specific information about SAT solver watch list implementations using SoA vs AoS data structures, you may need to look at specialized SAT solver papers or documentation from solvers like MiniSat, CaDiCaL, or other modern SAT solvers that discuss their data structure optimizations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8fa71b57-055a-4d0c-957e-78a340af150d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:39.453Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6e3f7024-e3e4-4f07-92c3-e2652