{"parentUuid":"94d176ce-ef8c-403b-bc32-e80364673bed","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01R7qDEFj6iHYgrng2KXT5Gx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y8Rzj3Q2fQ7pbdE1EGNH7v","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"two watched literals\" cache locality memory bandwidth SAT solver implementation"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1268,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4475,"cache_read_input_tokens":21834,"output_tokens":314,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4475},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1268,"output_tokens":314,"cache_read_input_tokens":21834,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4475,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4475,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaozbRpmh5KpsE8Gvjgdy","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"905ccf16-9d5e-4116-a019-d570c04ba17c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:46.046Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"720cf97e-364f-4b89-bd5d-10ce16445ec1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AmbALm1rE1fJd4ncZqFFNK","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CaDiCaL Kissat memory layout cache efficiency clause database 2024\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CADICAL, KISSAT, PARACOOBA, PLINGELING and TREENGELING\",\"url\":\"https://kfazekas.github.io/papers/BiereFazekasFleuryHeisinger-SAT-Competition-2020-solvers.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CaDiCaL, Gimsatul, IsaSAT and Kissat Entering the SAT Competition 2024\",\"url\":\"https://cca.informatik.uni-freiburg.de/papers/BiereFallerFazekasFleuryFroleyksPollitt-SAT-Competition-2024-solvers.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CaDiCaL 2.0 | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-65627-9_7\"},{\"title\":\"Kissat vs. Cadical vs. Cadical · Issue #22 · arminbiere/cadical\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arminbiere/cadical/issues/22\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · arminbiere/cadical\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arminbiere/cadical/releases\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - arminbiere/kissat · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arminbiere/kissat\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] CADICAL, KISSAT, PARACOOBA, PLINGELING and ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/CADICAL,-KISSAT,-PARACOOBA,-PLINGELING-and-Entering-Biere-Fazekas/3484ea875446d8b0f7bb2ff04e25cbfd34248f14\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] CADICAL, KISSAT, PARACOOBA Entering the SAT ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/CADICAL,-KISSAT,-PARACOOBA-Entering-the-SAT-2021-Biere-Fleury/5d07c083e16b868ae5000918189a5aa6866c2b20\"},{\"title\":\"kissat/NEWS.md at master · arminbiere/kissat\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arminbiere/kissat/blob/master/NEWS.md\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Solver Etudes I | Hey There Buddo!\",\"url\":\"https://www.philipzucker.com/python_sat/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about CaDiCaL and Kissat related to memory layout, cache efficiency, and clause handling in 2024:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Memory Layout and Cache Efficiency:**\n\nKissat was written in C to keep full control of memory layout, with all important algorithms ported from CaDiCaL and their effectiveness reconfirmed in a fresh implementation.\n\nKissat is more compact in memory usage and often faster on individual instances compared to CaDiCaL, though it lacks support for basic incremental features such as assumptions.\n\n**2024 Updates:**\n\nRecent information about Kissat is available in the SAT Competition 2024 solver description from Armin Biere and others, published in the Department of Computer Science Report Series B.\n\nKissat dominated the main track of the SAT Competition 2024, winning 3 gold medals with core techniques including congruence closure, clausal equivalence sweeping, bounded variable addition, and vivification.\n\n**Clause Database:**\n\nThe search results reference technical improvements to clause handling and vivification in both solvers, though specific details about clause database memory layout optimization aren't extensively detailed in these particular results. For more technical details on clause database optimization, you may want to consult the full CAV 2024 paper on CaDiCaL 2.0 or the Kissat source code repository directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"25b245e7-cf6e-473c-b902-5b03ad7ab48f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:49.648Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"720cf97e-364f-4b89-bd5d-10ce16445ec1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"1f67c988-f977-411c-a109-f6c732a08244","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KmZdCjo9DL8yUjZ2vp9oPV","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT solver profile guided optimization PGO LTO compilation 2024 2025 benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"From Profiling to Optimization: Unveiling the Profile Guided Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.16649v1\"},{\"title\":\"Link-Time Optimization (LTO), Profile-Guided Optimization (PGO), Post-Link Optimization (PLO) benchmark results · Issue #765 · linebender/resvg\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/linebender/resvg/issues/765\"},{\"title\":\"Using Profile-Guided Optimization (PGO) to optimize Factorio's performance - Factorio Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.factorio.com/viewtopic.php?t=114826\"},{\"title\":\"Profile-Guided Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.graalvm.org/latest/reference-manual/native-image/optimizations-and-performance/PGO/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - the-risk-taker/lto-pgo: This project demonstrates and compares the effects of Link Time Optimization (LTO) and Profile-Guided Optimization (PGO) · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/the-risk-taker/lto-pgo\"},{\"title\":\"awesome-pgo/article/article.md at main · zamazan4ik/awesome-pgo\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/zamazan4ik/awesome-pgo/blob/main/article/article.md\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - zamazan4ik/awesome-pgo: Various materials about Profile Guided Optimization and other similar stuff like AutoFDO, Bolt, etc. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ZaMaZaN4iK/awesome-pgo\"},{\"title\":\"GCC Profile Guided Optimizations\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/profile-guided-optimizations-in-gcc\"},{\"title\":\"kindatechnical() | Optimizing Compiler Design - Profile-Guided Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://kindatechnical.com/optimizing-compiler-design/profile-guided-optimization.html\"},{\"title\":\"Faster with Compiler Optimization Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/compiler-optimization-report-news-2025.html\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding SAT solver optimization with PGO, LTO, and compilation benchmarks in 2024-2025:\n\n## Profile-Guided Optimization (PGO) Overview\n\nPGO is a compiler optimization technique that uses information from a sample run of the program to guide subsequent compilations, involving compilation with special instrumentation, running it with typical workloads to generate a profile, and then recompiling using the generated profile data.\n\n## Recent Research and Developments\n\nAyupov et al. (2024) tackled profile staleness challenges by combining multi-level hash matching with flow-conservation inference, using a two-stage hashing strategy to efficiently align stale profile data with new code versions.\n\nInstrumentation-based profiling typically incurs 10–50% runtime overhead during profiling, with hardware-sampling regimes achieving similar performance improvements at less than 2% overhead, with LBR-based PGO yielding up to 93% of the gain of full instrumentation on C++ code.\n\n## PGO and LTO Combination\n\nPGO is usually applied after LTO since LTO is typically easier to enable and brings significant performance and/or binary size improvements, and PGO does not replace LTO but complements it.\n\n## Benchmark Results\n\nAverage PGO speedups on real production benchmarks include 5–10% on integer workloads with instrumentation, up to 30% in hot kernel loops, and 3–7% with LBR hardware sampling.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific information about SAT solvers with PGO/LTO optimization benchmarks. For targeted SAT solver studies, you may need to search academic databases or compiler-specific documentation.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"56ae8a78-3b1d-485d-8957-edd9af82e668","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:52.130Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1f67c988-f977-411c-a109-f6c732a08244","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"905ccf16-9d5e-4116-a019-d570c04ba17c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y8Rzj3Q2fQ7pbdE1EGNH7v","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"two watched literals\" cache locality memory bandwidth SAT solver implementation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The \\\"2 watched literal\\\" algorithm used in sat solvers (here is a random blog pos... | Hacker News\",\"url\":\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=18236555\"},{\"title\":\"memory layout | Wonderings of a SAT geek\",\"url\":\"https://www.msoos.org/tag/memory-layout/\"},{\"title\":\"A Verified SAT Solver with Watched Literals Using Imperative HOL Mathias Fleury\",\"url\":\"https://people.mpi-inf.mpg.de/~mfleury/sat_twl.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Modern SAT solvers: fast, neat and underused (part 3 of N) — The Coding Nest\",\"url\":\"https://codingnest.com/modern-sat-solvers-fast-neat-and-underused-part-3-of-n/\"},{\"title\":\"Changing the Watchlist Scheme | Wonderings of a SAT geek\",\"url\":\"https://www.msoos.org/2011/01/changing-the-watchlist-scheme/\"},{\"title\":\"Chaff: Engineering an Efficient SAT Solver Matthew W.Moskewicz,\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~emc/spring06/home_files/zchaff4ed.new.ppt\"},{\"title\":\"Cache Conscious Data Structures for Boolean Satisfiability ...\",\"url\":\"https://people.eng.unimelb.edu.au/pstuckey/papers/jsat09.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Verified SAT Solver with Watched Literals Using ...\",\"url\":\"https://matryoshka-project.github.io/matryoshka2018/slides/Matryoskha-2018-Fleury-A-Verified-SAT-Solver.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Verified SAT Solver with Watched Literals Using Imperative HOL (Extended Abstract)\",\"url\":\"https://easychair.org/smart-slide/slide/8cW9\"},{\"title\":\"The 2-watching literal method. The watched literals are are x 6 and x 8... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/The-2-watching-literal-method-The-watched-literals-are-are-x-6-and-x-8-Variable-x-6_fig3_220565739\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the two watched literals algorithm and its relationship to cache locality and memory bandwidth in SAT solver implementations:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe 2 watched literals algorithm, pioneered by the Chaff solver, selects 2 literals from each clause (watched), and the clause is only visited when one of them would be removed from the clause (in other words, its variable takes the opposite polarity).\n\n## Performance in Practice\n\nThe algorithm is very efficient and is used in basically every SAT solver with minimal modifications. In the worst case it is no more efficient than the algorithm it replaced, but in practice it is hundreds of times more efficient.\n\n## Cache Locality Benefits\n\nChaff's philosophy emphasizes coding efficiency and optimizing data cache behavior. The algorithm provides significant cache-related advantages:\n\nStoring a literal in the watched lists effectively eliminates the memory read required to pull in the clause 50-90% of the time, and the locality of reference is far better on the watched lists than among randomly located clauses in memory, substantially improving the caching properties of the solver.\n\nL2 miss penalties are on the order of 100-200 cycles, and studies show that 60-70% of runtime is spent simply waiting for data to be read in.\n\n## Memory Bandwidth Optimization\n\nBy not updating clauses frequently, more memory bandwidth should be available, as less information needs to be moved back into main memory. Additionally, watchlists can be prefetched when a literal is propagated, helping to mitigate memory latency.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"ae34699b-b91c-4e9a-86cd-924b6723ff77","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:53.598Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"905ccf16-9d5e-4116-a019-d570c04ba17c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"94d176ce-ef8c-403b-bc32-e80364673bed","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E44peZwe1wStcFTLVrBRsu","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cache conscious clause learning conflict analysis prefetch SAT solver 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Conflict-Driven Clause Learning SAT Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/255409904_Conflict-Driven_Clause_Learning_SAT_Solvers\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-Driven Clause Learning SAT Solvers - cs.Princeton\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~zkincaid/courses/fall18/readings/SATHandbook-CDCL.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-driven clause learning (CDCL) SAT solvers — CS-E3220: Propositional satisfiability and SAT solvers documentation\",\"url\":\"https://users.aalto.fi/~tjunttil/2020-DP-AUT/notes-sat/cdcl.html\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-driven clause learning SAT solvers - Princeton University\",\"url\":\"https://collaborate.princeton.edu/en/publications/conflict-driven-clause-learning-sat-solvers/\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Conflict-Driven Clause Learning SAT Solvers | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Conflict-Driven-Clause-Learning-SAT-Solvers-Marques-Silva-Lynce/6899e192961ccf6e36a3d4a703cb1c840414a219\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Minisat v1.13-a SAT solver with conflict-clause minimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228943311_Minisat_v113-a_SAT_solver_with_conflict-clause_minimization\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-driven clause learning - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict-driven_clause_learning\"},{\"title\":\"Rethinking Clause Management for CDCL SAT Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.20829\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 4: Conflict-driven clause learning SAT solvers - Princeton University\",\"url\":\"https://collaborate.princeton.edu/en/publications/chapter-4-conflict-driven-clause-learning-sat-solvers/\"},{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2025\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=uUsL07BsMA\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about SAT solvers and clause learning, though the results don't specifically focus on cache-conscious design or prefetching optimization for SAT solvers.\n\nHere's what the search revealed:\n\n## Key Findings on SAT Solvers and Clause Learning (2024-2025)\n\nConflict-driven clause learning (CDCL) is an algorithm for solving the Boolean satisfiability problem (SAT). CDCL SAT solvers incorporate conflict analysis, lazy data structures, search restarts, conflict-driven heuristics and clause deletion strategies.\n\nRecent developments in SAT solving include:\n\n- **Clause Management**: CDCL solvers learn clauses during conflict analysis for search space pruning, but must periodically eliminate less useful learned clauses due to memory constraints and computational overhead, making clause management critical for solver efficiency.\n\n- **Machine Learning Integration**: Recent research investigates predicting branching orders using graph neural networks as a preprocessing step to CDCL SAT solvers, showing significant gains can be made in existing CDCL solvers by providing good initial branching.\n\n- **Competition Results**: LLM-based solver evolution starting from SAT Competition 2024 codebases produced solvers that outperformed the human-designed winners of the SAT Competition 2025.\n\nThe search results don't contain specific information about cache-conscious optimization or prefetch strategies in SAT solvers. For more detailed information on those topics, you may want to search for papers specifically focused on hardware optimization and memory access patterns in SAT solvers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"684dfdf7-ebe9-4b62-80a4-9786c4831025","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:53.790Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"94d176ce-ef8c-403b-bc32-e80364673bed","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
