{"parentUuid":"28cf481a-0ae3-4379-b72b-b84323a1b922","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01S7oR3FRTh5x9BipDgmpjBJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011NVwn5cxgouuTT28Ykp5jw","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"jemalloc tcmalloc SAT solver allocation cache benchmark CDCL"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1145,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4469,"cache_read_input_tokens":36569,"output_tokens":307,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4469},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1145,"output_tokens":307,"cache_read_input_tokens":36569,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4469,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4469,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caozf4DNVfXWw5UBGVLwd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"47df93eb-fc54-4c53-8b3c-6a6d0cb367b0","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:35.001Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"8f5ca483-2674-411b-be38-aa3242df873f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UzK75PHrpMJkBKBtYsHRd4","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"compact-table\" sparse bitset propagator cache friendly extensional constraint\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[1604.06641] Compact-Table: Efficiently Filtering Table Constraints with Reversible Sparse Bit-Sets\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.06641\"},{\"title\":\"Compact-Table: Efficiently Filtering Table Constraints with Reversible Sparse Bit-Sets | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/301878220_Compact-Table_Efficiently_Filtering_Table_Constraints_with_Reversible_Sparse_Bit-Sets\"},{\"title\":\"[1604.06641] Compact-Table: Efficiently Filtering Table Constraints with Reversible Sparse Bit-Sets\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1604.06641\"},{\"title\":\"Compact-Table: Efficiently Filtering Table Constraints with Reversible Sparse Bit-Sets | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-44953-1_14\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Compact-Table: Efficiently Filtering Table Constraints with Reversible Sparse Bit-Sets\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/73083992/Compact_Table_Efficiently_Filtering_Table_Constraints_with_Reversible_Sparse_Bit_Sets\"},{\"title\":\"Making Compact-Table Compact | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-98334-9_14\"},{\"title\":\"[1604.06641v1] Compact-Table: Efficiently Filtering Table Constraints with Reversible Sparse Bit-Sets\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.06641v1\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Compact-Table: Efficiently Filtering Table Constraints with Reversible Sparse Bit-Sets | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Compact-Table:-Efficiently-Filtering-Table-with-Demeulenaere-Hartert/88368d5fe6325a60cd6e10e39e1745a85039f547\"},{\"title\":\"The extensional constraint | DIAL.pr - BOREAL\",\"url\":\"https://dial.uclouvain.be/pr/boreal/object/boreal:252859\"},{\"title\":\"Compact-Table: Efficiently Filtering Table Constraints with Reversible Sparse Bit-Sets | DIAL.pr - BOREAL\",\"url\":\"https://dial.uclouvain.be/pr/boreal/en/object/boreal:177731/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about \"Compact-Table\" and the related concepts:\n\n## Compact-Table Algorithm\n\nCompact-Table (CT) is a bitwise algorithm to enforce Generalized Arc Consistency (GAC) on table constraints. This algorithm is the default propagator for table constraints in or-tools and OscaR, two publicly available CP solvers.\n\n## Key Features\n\nCT has been improved with the introduction of residues, resetting operations and a data-structure called reversible sparse bit-set, used to maintain tables of supports (following the idea of tabular reduction): tuples are invalidated incrementally on value removals by means of bit-set operations.\n\nTable constraints, also called extensional constraints, explicitly express the allowed combinations of values for the variables they involve as sequences of tuples, which are called tables.\n\n## Performance\n\nCT outperforms state-of-the-art algorithms STR2, STR3, GAC4R, MDD4R and AC5-TC on standard benchmarks.\n\n## Cache-Friendly Characteristics\n\nThe compact-table propagator for table constraints appears to be a strong candidate for inclusion into any constraint solver due to its efficiency and simplicity. The algorithm's use of bitwise operations and sparse data structures contributes to its computational efficiency and memory optimization.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"bdf2a5e7-2792-4089-bcaf-15b8e15ff779","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:38.447Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8f5ca483-2674-411b-be38-aa3242df873f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9fd945a6-f905-4778-b452-21e6ec67361d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FfBL6p9KyYETTKsWTUBgoy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT 2024 SAT 2025 conference paper memory data structure clause efficient\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SAT: Theory and Applications of Satisfiability Testing 2027 2026 2025 ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=2551\"},{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2024\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/file/64587794695be22545d91c838243fcf8-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SAT 2024\",\"url\":\"https://satisfiability.org/SAT24/\"},{\"title\":\"Lazy Clause Exchange Policy for Parallel SAT Solvers | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-09284-3_15\"},{\"title\":\"Optimizing local search-based partial MaxSAT solving via ...\",\"url\":\"http://scis.scichina.com/en/2025/122101.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"dblp: SAT 2024\",\"url\":\"https://dblp.org/db/conf/sat/sat2024.html\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Competition 2024\",\"url\":\"https://satcompetition.github.io/2024/\"},{\"title\":\"Cache Performance of SAT Solvers: a Case Study for Efficient Implementation of Algorithms | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-540-24605-3_22\"},{\"title\":\"CFP\",\"url\":\"https://easychair.org/cfp/SAT2024\"},{\"title\":\"SAT 2023 - SAT 2023\",\"url\":\"http://satisfiability.org/SAT23/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are some relevant findings related to SAT conference papers from 2024 and 2025 that involve memory, data structures, clauses, and efficiency:\n\n## SAT 2024 Conference Papers\n\nOne notable paper is \"Parallel Clause Sharing Strategy Based on Graph Structure of SAT Problem\" by Yoichiro Iida, Tomohiro Sonobe, and Mary Inaba, which directly addresses clause management and data structures for efficient SAT solving.\n\nAdditionally, research on managing learnt clauses in parallel, memory-shared SAT solvers proposes lazy policies to exchange clauses between solvers.\n\n## Memory and Data Structure Considerations\n\nResearch evaluates the cache performance of different SAT solvers as a case study for the efficient implementation of SAT algorithms, highlighting the importance of memory efficiency in SAT solving.\n\n## 2025 Research\n\nRecent work on optimizing SAT/MaxSAT solving addresses memory constraints, noting that some instances cannot be handled due to insufficient video memory, indicating ongoing challenges with memory efficiency in modern SAT solvers.\n\nThe search results show that memory management, clause data structures, and algorithmic efficiency remain active areas of research in the SAT conference community.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"906cf6bd-1111-4f3c-9d9a-8aa2139b0eb9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:39.041Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9fd945a6-f905-4778-b452-21e6ec67361d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"28cf481a-0ae3-4379-b72b-b84323a1b922","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012r7odxwmqu2NmjjK7RGt1y","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"huge pages transparent hugepages SAT solver CP solver performance Linux\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Transparent Hugepages: measuring the performance impact - The mole is digging\",\"url\":\"https://alexandrnikitin.github.io/blog/transparent-hugepages-measuring-the-performance-impact/\"},{\"title\":\"5.2. Huge Pages and Transparent Huge Pages | Performance Tuning Guide | Red Hat Enterprise Linux | 6 | Red Hat Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/6/html/performance_tuning_guide/s-memory-transhuge\"},{\"title\":\"Transparent Hugepage Support — The Linux Kernel documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/transhuge.html\"},{\"title\":\"Examining Huge Pages or Transparent Huge Pages performance | Red Hat Developer\",\"url\":\"https://developers.redhat.com/blog/2014/03/10/examining-huge-pages-or-transparent-huge-pages-performance\"},{\"title\":\"How to use, monitor, and disable transparent hugepages in Red Hat Enterprise Linux ? - Red Hat Customer Portal\",\"url\":\"https://access.redhat.com/solutions/46111\"},{\"title\":\"Linux Huge Pages and Web Performance - GetPageSpeed\",\"url\":\"https://www.getpagespeed.com/server-setup/linux-huge-pages-and-web-performance\"},{\"title\":\"Turning off transparent hugepages - Install, Configure and Update - CryoSPARC Discuss\",\"url\":\"https://discuss.cryosparc.com/t/turning-off-transparent-hugepages/15819\"},{\"title\":\"Configuring HugePages for Oracle on Linux (x86-64) - ORACLE-BASE\",\"url\":\"https://oracle-base.com/articles/linux/configuring-huge-pages-for-oracle-on-linux-64\"},{\"title\":\"Huge Pages or Transparent Huge Pages in Context of Exadata | exadata\",\"url\":\"https://blogs.oracle.com/exadata/huge-pages-or-transparent-huge-pages-in-context-of-exadata\"},{\"title\":\"7.4. Configuring Transparent Huge Pages | Performance Tuning Guide | Red Hat Enterprise Linux | 7 | Red Hat Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/7/html/performance_tuning_guide/sect-red_hat_enterprise_linux-performance_tuning_guide-configuring_transparent_huge_pages\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about huge pages, transparent hugepages, and SAT/CP solver performance on Linux. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Transparent Hugepages Overview\n\nTransparent Hugepages (THP) manage large pages automatically and transparently for applications, reducing TLB misses and making page table walking cheaper with no changes required on the application side.\n\n## How THP Works\n\nThe transparent hugepage mechanism will automatically use larger pages for dynamically allocated memory. A khugepaged kernel thread occasionally attempts to substitute smaller pages being used currently with a hugepage allocation, thus maximizing THP usage.\n\n## Performance Considerations\n\nThe benefits include reducing TLB misses and making page table walking cheaper. However, there are tradeoffs. Despite being a background thread, khugepaged locks pages it works with, which could cause latency spikes. Additionally, the OS splits large pages into regular ones for operations like swap, which could degrade performance and increase memory fragmentation.\n\n## Configuration Options\n\nThe values for /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled can be \"always\" (always use THP), \"madvise\" (only use THP for processes that explicitly request it), or \"never\" (disable THP).\n\nThe search results provide general information about transparent hugepages and Linux performance tuning, but don't contain specific benchmarking data comparing SAT solvers or Constraint Programming solvers with transparent hugepages enabled versus disabled.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7641cf1f-2b88-4006-9fbe-740bbd8fd8fa","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:42.052Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"28cf481a-0ae3-4379-b72b-b84323a1b922","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"47df93eb-fc54-4c53-8b3c-6a6d0cb367b0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"ae3590b7e2f938057","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011NVwn5cxgouuTT28Ykp5jw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"jemalloc tcmalloc SAT solver allocation cache benchmark CDCL\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"libmalloc, jemalloc, tcmalloc, mimalloc - Exploring Different Memory Allocators - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/frosnerd/libmalloc-jemalloc-tcmalloc-mimalloc-exploring-different-memory-allocators-4lp3\"},{\"title\":\"C++ High-Memory Allocation Performance: tcmalloc vs. jemalloc Comparison – Benefits, Insights & Benchmarks\",\"url\":\"https://linuxvox.com/blog/c-memory-allocation-mechanism-performance-comparison-tcmalloc-vs-jemalloc/\"},{\"title\":\"Memory Allocator Benchmarks: mimalloc vs jemalloc vs tcmalloc in 2026 | NexusFIX News\",\"url\":\"https://stratcraft.ai/nexusfix/news/memory-allocator-benchmarks-2026\"},{\"title\":\"Testing Memory Allocators: ptmalloc2 vs tcmalloc vs hoard vs jemalloc While Trying to Simulate Real-World Loads - IT Hare on Soft.ware\",\"url\":\"http://ithare.com/testing-memory-allocators-ptmalloc2-tcmalloc-hoard-jemalloc-while-trying-to-simulate-real-world-loads/\"},{\"title\":\"jemalloc vs malloc vs tcmalloc: Why Your Server's Default Allocator Is Killing P99 Latency - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/kunal_d6a8fea2309e1571ee7/jemalloc-vs-malloc-vs-tcmalloc-why-your-servers-default-allocator-is-killing-p99-latency-6l8\"},{\"title\":\"jemalloc vs tcmalloc vs mimalloc: Which to choose?\",\"url\":\"https://beefed.ai/en/choose-memory-allocator-jemalloc-tcmalloc-mimalloc\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - microsoft/mimalloc: mimalloc is a compact general purpose allocator with excellent performance. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/microsoft/mimalloc\"},{\"title\":\"On the Impact of Memory Allocation on High-Performance Query Processing\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.01135\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking Ruby's Heap: malloc, tcmalloc, jemalloc — Engineering Blog\",\"url\":\"https://engineering.appfolio.com/appfolio-engineering/2018/2/1/benchmarking-rubys-heap-malloc-tcmalloc-jemalloc\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarks of the Lockless Memory Allocator\",\"url\":\"https://locklessinc.com/benchmarks_allocator.shtml\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about jemalloc, tcmalloc, SAT solver allocation, cache benchmarking, and CDCL. Here are the results:\n\nThe search returned multiple articles comparing jemalloc and tcmalloc memory allocators across various benchmarks and workloads:\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. **Allocator Comparison**: jemalloc, mimalloc, and tcmalloc manage to keep overhead below 30% for most allocation sizes, making them preferred over default system allocators.\n\n2. **Throughput Characteristics**: tcmalloc's thread-local caches outperform jemalloc by ~22% in throughput for workloads with 100M allocations of 64-byte blocks, while jemalloc's arenas scale better with many threads, delivering 30% higher throughput and lower tail latency.\n\n3. **Query Processing**: jemalloc performs best in high-performance query processing workloads.\n\n4. **Memory Efficiency**: Fragmentation over time favors jemalloc for long-running processes, as tcmalloc's size classes and page management are optimized for throughput while jemalloc's are optimized for memory efficiency over time.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific information about SAT solvers, CDCL (Conflict-Driven Clause Learning), or benchmarks specifically comparing these allocators in the context of SAT solving. For more targeted information on memory allocation in SAT solvers, you may need to search for academic papers on SAT solver optimization or performance.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"566480e9-2726-4fcf-9b1f-ade1a94dec0d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:42.840Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"47df93eb-fc54-4c53-8b3c-6a6d0cb367b0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
